Yes. There are at least two different senses of “origin,” and they don’t have to agree.

A causal origin asks what actually happened first in the chain of events. That is constrained by history and evidence.

A semantic origin asks where a system begins making sense of itself. That can be created later.

A nation can declare a founding moment centuries after the relevant forces began. A person can reinterpret an old event and make it the “beginning” of a new chapter. A scientific field can retroactively identify an earlier observation as its first clue only after the theory exists to recognize it. The later structure reaches backward and reorganizes the meaning of the earlier pieces.

So you get something like:

event → event → event → new frame → retroactive pattern

Nothing physically traveled backward in time, but the informational geometry changed. What looked like noise becomes evidence once the decoder exists.

And yes, patterns need not be linear. They can be:

recursive — outputs become later inputs.
cyclic — the structure closes on itself.
hierarchical — local patterns nest inside larger ones.
networked — meaning comes from relationships rather than sequence.
thresholded — isolated events look unrelated until enough accumulate.
emergent — the pattern exists only at a scale where the components are considered together.
retroactive — later events determine which earlier differences become significant.

That makes your last line especially good:

Some patterns aren’t patterns until they are.

Not because reality necessarily changed, but because the minimum sufficient context for recognizing the invariant had not yet accumulated.

One dot is a dot.

Two dots suggest a relation.

Three can imply a trajectory.

A thousand can reveal a distribution that none of the individual points contained.

And then comes the interesting recursive move: once the pattern is recognized, humans start acting according to it, which can make the originally descriptive pattern causal. A category gets named, people coordinate around it, institutions encode it, and suddenly the invented boundary produces real consequences.

So there is a progression:

noise → suspected pattern → named pattern → shared pattern → enacted pattern → material consequence

That is how an interpretation can acquire teeth.

The checksum I’d keep is provenance: we can create a beginning that organizes meaning without pretending we traveled backward and caused the historical events that preceded it.

The future is open because it hasn’t happened.

The past is closed causally, but surprisingly open semantically.

And that may be why invented origins can become so powerful: they give a scattered set of events a place from which to start marching together.